1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:05,616
Last semester, my room almost broke up over
two hundred and sixty six rupees.

2
00:00:06,066 --> 00:00:07,852
Not because anyone was cheating.

3
00:00:08,302 --> 00:00:11,284
Because nobody could remember who paid for the
Wi-Fi.

4
00:00:11,734 --> 00:00:15,385
Three of us were sure we had paid it. The
fourth one actually had.

5
00:00:16,135 --> 00:00:17,070
Sound familiar?

6
00:00:17,520 --> 00:00:21,328
Small payments, made by different people, all
month long.

7
00:00:27,078 --> 00:00:30,354
In this video I'll share five habits that
fixed it for us.

8
00:00:30,804 --> 00:00:33,105
They work with any app, or even a notebook.

9
00:00:33,555 --> 00:00:40,502
I'll show each one in Hisaab, an expense
splitting app I designed for students, so you
can see exactly how it looks.

10
00:00:41,252 --> 00:00:42,016
Here's the list.

11
00:00:42,466 --> 00:00:46,946
Stick around for habit four. It's the one that
saves friendships.

12
00:00:47,696 --> 00:00:50,309
Habit one. Log it the moment you pay.

13
00:00:50,759 --> 00:00:53,310
Not tonight, and not at the end of the month.

14
00:00:53,760 --> 00:01:00,126
Memory is the most expensive part of splitting
money. Every expense you add later is one you
might get wrong.

15
00:01:00,576 --> 00:01:08,650
We tested this in our room. Expenses added on
the spot were always right. Expenses added at
night were wrong about one time in five.

16
00:01:09,399 --> 00:01:12,159
In Hisaab, adding takes about ten seconds.

17
00:01:12,609 --> 00:01:15,504
Open your room's group, and tap the plus
button.

18
00:01:15,954 --> 00:01:20,259
The amount pad opens first, because the amount
is the one thing you always know.

19
00:01:20,709 --> 00:01:24,932
Paid by defaults to you, and the split
defaults to everyone in the group.

20
00:01:25,382 --> 00:01:28,782
So most of the time, you type a number and hit
save.

21
00:01:29,532 --> 00:01:32,564
Habit two. Split fairly, not equally.

22
00:01:33,014 --> 00:01:36,456
Equal splits feel simple, but they quietly
annoy people.

23
00:01:36,906 --> 00:01:43,467
If Aarav was at the library during the Maggi
run, he shouldn't pay for it. And he shouldn't
have to argue about it either.

24
00:01:43,917 --> 00:01:47,329
Fair means everyone pays for what they were
part of. That's it.

25
00:01:48,079 --> 00:01:52,950
So the split screen starts with everyone
ticked, and you untick whoever wasn't there.

26
00:01:53,400 --> 00:01:58,649
Hisaab recalculates instantly. A hundred and
twenty each, three people.

27
00:01:59,099 --> 00:02:04,726
And it shows that the total still adds up to
three sixty, so nobody has to double check the
maths.

28
00:02:05,176 --> 00:02:10,523
For rent or a trip, you can switch to exact
amounts, shares, or percentages.

29
00:02:11,273 --> 00:02:13,757
Habit three. Pick one settle day.

30
00:02:14,207 --> 00:02:15,372
Ours is Sunday evening.

31
00:02:15,822 --> 00:02:21,219
When settling is a routine, asking for money
stops being personal. It's just Sunday.

32
00:02:21,669 --> 00:02:28,178
It also keeps the amounts small. Settling
ninety rupees every week is easier than five
hundred at month end.

33
00:02:28,928 --> 00:02:31,939
On settle day, open home and tap settle up.

34
00:02:32,389 --> 00:02:36,642
You see exactly who you're paying, their UPI
ID, and what it's for.

35
00:02:37,092 --> 00:02:42,856
Pick any UPI app and confirm. The money goes
bank to bank. Hisaab never holds it.

36
00:02:43,306 --> 00:02:48,507
Your balance updates straight away, and you
can clear the next person in the same sitting.

37
00:02:49,257 --> 00:02:53,536
Habit four, the friendship saver. Let the app
do the asking.

38
00:02:53,986 --> 00:02:59,158
Nobody wants to be the person sending, hey,
about that hundred and twenty.

39
00:02:59,608 --> 00:03:05,115
But a neutral reminder, with the exact amount
and a pay link, doesn't feel like nagging.

40
00:03:05,565 --> 00:03:08,973
The app becomes the bad guy, and you get to
stay the friend.

41
00:03:09,723 --> 00:03:14,347
Tap remind, and pick a tone. Friendly, direct,
or funny.

42
00:03:14,797 --> 00:03:18,762
The message goes from your own WhatsApp, so
you decide when it's sent.

43
00:03:19,212 --> 00:03:24,841
Hisaab never sends automatic reminders behind
your back. That was a deliberate design
choice.

44
00:03:25,591 --> 00:03:27,858
Habit five. Know where the money goes.

45
00:03:28,308 --> 00:03:30,976
Here's a sample month for one student in our
room.

46
00:03:31,426 --> 00:03:37,140
Food and chai is almost half. Travel is next.
Room and bills are smaller than you'd think.

47
00:03:37,890 --> 00:03:40,075
Hisaab shows this as monthly insights.

48
00:03:40,525 --> 00:03:43,119
And the most useful part is the nudge at the
bottom.

49
00:03:43,569 --> 00:03:50,186
Late night food was the biggest category, so
it suggests a monthly limit. You can take it
or ignore it.

50
00:03:50,936 --> 00:03:54,964
Before we wrap up, three mistakes we made, so
you don't have to.

51
00:03:55,414 --> 00:04:01,730
One. Rounding everything up. Ten rupees here
and there adds up, and someone always notices.

52
00:04:02,180 --> 00:04:08,357
Two. Letting one person become the room's
bank. They end up owed thousands, and too shy
to ask.

53
00:04:08,807 --> 00:04:15,290
Three. Settling only when someone is broke. By
then the amounts are big, and the conversation
is awkward.

54
00:04:16,040 --> 00:04:21,874
All three have the same fix. Small amounts,
logged right away, settled on a fixed day.

55
00:04:22,624 --> 00:04:23,450
Quick recap.

56
00:04:23,900 --> 00:04:30,943
Log it when you pay. Split fairly. Settle on
one day. Let the app ask. And know where it
goes.

57
00:04:31,393 --> 00:04:37,699
None of these are about maths. They're about
making money boring, so friendships don't have
to absorb it.

58
00:04:38,449 --> 00:04:42,114
If this helped, subscribe for more student
money tips.

59
00:04:42,564 --> 00:04:47,433
Next, I'll show how I designed Hisaab in one
week, from research to prototype.

60
00:04:47,883 --> 00:04:50,256
Link on screen now. See you there.
